{"parentUuid":"d8ccbc95-07a4-40b9-abe6-385b8e521dad","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ad2d4257a6c907b3e","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfY32J8eDC6at8vkWHUxT","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Q6twsaCVQsTAoQ5vWmBBRm","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-skills/64f15158-6a4e-5bdf-8a90-7f2bd7b05b82/scratchpad/r2/engine-skill-framework/ && grep -n -i -E \"physic|simulat|crowd|vehicle|determin|rollback|fluid|cloth|destruct|middleware|jolt|havok\" docs/00-design-principles.md docs/06-gap-analysis.md docs/07-research-plan.md | head -80"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7476,"cache_read_input_tokens":90906,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7476,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01Q6twsaCVQsTAoQ5vWmBBRm":{"command":"cd /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-skills/64f15158-6a4e-5bdf-8a90-7f2bd7b05b82/scratchpad/r2/engine-skill-framework/ && grep -n -i -E \"physic|simulat|crowd|vehicle|determin|rollback|fluid|cloth|destruct|middleware|jolt|havok\" docs/00-design-principles.md docs/06-gap-analysis.md docs/07-research-plan.md | head -80"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CfY32HkZtRDzzp7pbKwgt","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"fec62b10-c2ca-4add-a28b-3276801fb690","timestamp":"2026-09-29T16:20:56.593Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","userType":"external","entrypoint":"remote_mobile","cwd":"/home/user/skills","sessionId":"64f15158-6a4e-5bdf-8a90-7f2bd7b05b82","version":"2.1.284","gitBranch":"claude/engine-skill-framework-phase1"}
{"parentUuid":"fec62b10-c2ca-4add-a28b-3276801fb690","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"9bdfc07b-8ff1-4e95-9c88-de7440fd6192","agentId":"ad2d4257a6c907b3e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Q6twsaCVQsTAoQ5vWmBBRm","type":"tool_result","content":"docs/00-design-principles.md:9:1. **Distinct expertise.** It needs knowledge that a neighbor does not: literature, APIs, hardware behavior. For example, tire models and constraint solvers share a physics lead but not a literature, which is why vehicle physics is a separate skill.\ndocs/00-design-principles.md:23:| Cross-cutting | 11 | Disciplines that impose an obligation on every skill and review against it: research evidence, performance, testing, security, observability, crash diagnostics, determinism, hot reload, accessibility, API lifecycle, developer experience. |\ndocs/00-design-principles.md:35:**Workstreams** (foundation, platform, content, world, rendering, simulation, audio, online, gameplay, UI, tools, release, quality, performance, governance) group skills for delegated arbitration and staffing. A dispute inside one lead's subtree is settled by that lead. Only cross-workstream disputes reach `engine-architect` (`ARCH.ORG.escalation`).\ndocs/00-design-principles.md:42:- **No upward module links.** A runtime skill's code may only consume contracts at or below the layer of the module doing the consuming. A skill that ships several modules attributes each dependency to one of them. For example, `C-SER@C-SNAPSHOT` means the snapshot module (L2) needs serialization, while the L1 determinism-rules module does not.\ndocs/00-design-principles.md:57:- **End-to-end feature ownership.** One skill owns an environment or feature system end to end: representation, simulation, rendering and tool logic. Environment skills keep their render dependencies optional, so their simulation and query halves still build headless.\ndocs/00-design-principles.md:68:- **Scale/feature profiles.** A base profile (`minimal`, `min2d`, `lite3d`, `std3d`) plus add-ons (`openworld`, `online`, `aaa`, `xr`, `ugc`, `massim`, `sandbox`, `vehicles`, `team-large`).\ndocs/00-design-principles.md:87:| The frame is a task graph. Ordering comes from declared data access (`C-FRAME`), data moves through channels (`C-FLOW`), and the canonical simulation schedule and multi-rate domains are explicit. | A central `Update()` loop, or a fixed sequence of tick groups with barriers. | Many-core and hybrid CPUs need dependency-driven overlap. Phase barriers serialize the frame and hide races. |\ndocs/00-design-principles.md:90:| Hybrid object model: ECS where bulk homogeneous simulation benefits. Subsystem contracts are ECS-independent (handles plus batched streams) and bridged to ECS by adapters. | Deep inheritance hierarchies, or \"ECS for everything\". | ECS pays off only for bulk homogeneous access. Making physics, audio or transforms depend on ECS would prevent middleware integration and force ECS into minimal games. |\ndocs/00-design-principles.md:91:| The gameplay framework is built from composed components, systems and services. Control binding is data, and the control model is chosen per game. | Actor→Pawn→Controller \"possession\" adopted by habit; per-object `Tick()`. | Possession does not fit RTS, card, simulation or MMO games, and per-object ticking is a known scaling limit. |\ndocs/00-design-principles.md:94:| Simulation-to-render extraction is change-tracked, with delta upload into a persistent instance scene. Cost is O(changes). | Per-object render proxy mirrors. | Per-proxy synchronization scales with object count instead of change count. |\ndocs/00-design-principles.md:96:| All IO is asynchronous and staged (IO → decompress → fixup → upload → publish), with deadlines and cross-pool arbitration under one physical budget. | Synchronous loads; page-fault streaming; independent streaming pools that compete. | NVMe throughput requires deep queues. On UMA hardware, texture, geometry, audio and BVH pools share one memory. |\ndocs/00-design-principles.md:99:| Instrumentation, tests, budgets and determinism levels exist before features. Independent oracles judge agent-produced work. | Profiling and testing added late; implementers grading their own tests. | Performance claims need measurement, and autonomous implementers can game tests they control. |\ndocs/00-design-principles.md:114:| Input is split into devices (`C-DEVICE`) and actions (`C-INPUT`). The action schema and tick-stamped command frames exist on every target. | Servers simulate from networked input commands; device binding exists only on clients. |\ndocs/00-design-principles.md:119:| Determinism, snapshot/resimulate and replay are one cross-cutting skill with three contracts: `C-DET` (L1 rules), `C-SNAPSHOT` (L2) and `C-REPLAY` (L3). | The properties hold only if every participant follows the same protocol. The split keeps each module on its correct layer. |\ndocs/06-gap-analysis.md:19:| A4 | Cross-platform floating-point determinism in production | Most shipped rollback titles are same-platform or use fixed-point. Cross-platform float reproducibility depends on compilers, FMA contraction and transcendental functions. | determinism-replay, math-simd-numerics | Build a determinism test matrix (compilers × ISAs × flags) with state hashing. Decide per subsystem between fixed-point, restricted float and \"same binary only\". |\ndocs/06-gap-analysis.md:27:| A12 | LLM-driven NPCs | Latency, cost, safety/moderation, determinism and age-rating implications are largely unresolved. Evidence is mostly demos. | ai-behavior-perception | `experimental`. It is only allowed behind an optional contract with moderation (platform-services), a budget, and an offline fallback. It is never on a required path. |\ndocs/06-gap-analysis.md:43:| B6 | Physics engine | Integrate (Jolt, PhysX, Havok, Box2D v3) vs build | physics-architect | Determinism needs, source access, licensing, feature coverage, performance on target workloads. |\ndocs/06-gap-analysis.md:44:| B7 | Audio engine | Own mixer vs middleware (Wwise, FMOD) | audio-architect | Sound-designer workflow, licensing, platform support, spatial-audio feature needs. |\ndocs/06-gap-analysis.md:47:| B10 | Netcode model | Server-authoritative + prediction vs rollback vs lockstep vs hybrid | network-architect | Genre, player count, determinism budget, bandwidth. |\ndocs/06-gap-analysis.md:57:| B20 | Transform/LWC representation | 64-bit world positions everywhere vs cell-relative 32-bit + origin rebasing | world-architect, spatial-transforms | World size, physics/GPU precision, network quantization. |\ndocs/06-gap-analysis.md:60:| B23 | Physics middleware mode | In-house vs integrated middleware with an engine-side integration layer | physics-architect | Rewind/snapshot support, determinism level, source access (see PHY.ARCH.middleware-layer). |\ndocs/07-research-plan.md:7:- **Conferences and venues:** SIGGRAPH (papers and the *Advances in Real-Time Rendering* and *Open Problems* courses), GDC (programming, tools, production tracks; GDC Vault), High Performance Graphics, Eurographics / EGSR, I3D, SCA (simulation), Digital Dragons, Reboot Develop, CppCon / C++Now (systems), ACM TOG / CGF journals, IEEE TVCG.\ndocs/07-research-plan.md:25:| **Lighting, shadows, GI, RT, PT** | direct-lighting-shadows, global-illumination, ray-tracing-infrastructure, path-tracing | ReSTIR (Bitterli et al., SIGGRAPH 2020); ReSTIR GI (Ouyang et al., HPG 2021); GRIS / ReSTIR PT (Lin et al., SIGGRAPH 2022); \"A Gentle Introduction to ReSTIR\" (SIGGRAPH 2023 course); Lumen (SIGGRAPH 2022 Advances); MegaLights (SIGGRAPH 2025 Advances); Virtual Shadow Maps (UE docs and talks); DDGI (Majercik et al., JCGT 2019); neural radiance caching (Müller et al., 2021); opacity micromaps and SER specs; cluster/partitioned acceleration-structure extensions and RTX Mega Geometry material; *Ray Tracing Gems I & II*; *Physically Based Rendering* 4th ed. (Pharr, Jakob, Humphreys). |\ndocs/07-research-plan.md:26:| **Materials & color** | material-system, post-color-hdr | OpenPBR spec; MaterialX; Substrate (UE docs/talks); ACES 2.0 documentation (Academy); OpenColorIO; ITU-R BT.2100 and SMPTE ST 2084 (PQ); HDR game-output guidance from platform holders; \"Physically Based Shading in Theory and Practice\" SIGGRAPH courses. |\ndocs/07-research-plan.md:31:| **Physics** | physics-architect and physics experts | Jolt Physics (Rouwe, GDC 2022 \"Architecting Jolt Physics for Horizon Forbidden West\"); Box2D v3 solver notes (Catto — author of record); XPBD (Macklin et al., 2016) and \"Small Steps in Physics Simulation\" (SCA 2019); PhysX 5 and Havok documentation; Chaos destruction docs; Game Physics Pearls; Ericson, *Real-Time Collision Detection* (foundational). |\ndocs/07-research-plan.md:34:| **Networking** | network-architect and network experts | \"Overwatch Gameplay Architecture and Netcode\" (GDC 2017); \"I Shot You First\" (Halo: Reach, GDC 2011); Rocket League physics networking (GDC 2018); GGPO / rollback literature; Tribes networking model (Frohnmayer & Gift — foundational); Valve Source multiplayer networking docs; QUIC (RFC 9000) and WebTransport; Gaffer on Games (author-of-record, graded T3); UE Iris replication docs; spatially sharded server talks (treat as `emerging`). |","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"a682b559-3596-4507-bf34-0bd0c0ff936b","timestamp":"2026-09-29T16:20:56.642Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"fec62b10-c2ca-4add-a28b-3276801fb690","userType":"external","entrypoint":"remote_mobile","cwd":"/home/user/skills","sessionId":"64f15158-6a4e-5bdf-8a90-7f2bd7b05b82","version":"2.1.284","gitBranch":"claude/engine-skill-framework-phase1"}
{"parentUuid":"a682b559-3596-4507-bf34-0bd0c0ff936b","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ad2d4257a6c907b3e","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfY32J8eDC6at8vkWHUxT","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Gg3L4zRB2UH8Br7TaU1ygf","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-skills/64f15158-6a4e-5bdf-8a90-7f2bd7b05b82/scratchpad/r2/engine-skill-framework/ && python3 -c \"\nimport json\nfor f in ['radar','legacy-patterns','critics','crosscutting','milestones','seed-map']:\n  d=json.load(open('data/%s.json'%f))\n  s=json.dumps(d)\n  import re\n  print('=====',f)\n  # print entries mentioning physics terms\n  def walk(o):\n    if isinstance(o,list):\n      for i in o: yield from walk(i)\n    elif isinstance(o,dict):\n      yield o\n      for v in o.values(): yield from walk(v)\n  for o in walk(d):\n    t=json.dumps(o)\n    if len(t)<900 and re.search(r'physic|PHY\\.|crowd|vehicle|fluid|cloth|destruct|rollback|massim|simulation|rigid|character-phys',t,re.I):\n      print(t)\n\" | head -120"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7476,"cache_read_input_tokens":90906,"output_tokens":3680,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":3073},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7476},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3680,"cache_read_input_tokens":90906,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7476,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7476,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01Gg3L4zRB2UH8Br7TaU1ygf":{"command":"cd /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-skills/64f15158-6a4e-5bdf-8a90-7f2bd7b05b82/s